---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Rule from "../components/Rule.astro";
import Distribution from "../components/Distribution.astro";
import ProvinceSplit from "../components/ProvinceSplit.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
const zrows = [
  ["South Africa", "616", "1:87,951", "10,814"],
  ["Australia", "16", "1:1,687,231", "81,699"],
  ["United Arab Emirates", "6", "1:1,527,046", "51,993"],
  ["United States", "3", "1:120,819,644", "1,331,040"],
  ["Botswana", "1", "1:2,186,929", "30,250"],
  ["Ireland", "1", "1:4,708,939", "29,543"],
  ["England", "1", "1:55,718,059", "489,080"],
  ["Saudi Arabia", "1", "1:30,855,817", "63,028"],
  ["Turkey", "1", "1:77,821,422", "191,047"],
];
const srows = [
  ["South Africa", "52,009", "1:1,042", "—"],
  ["Namibia", "1,020", "—", "—"],
  ["England", "175", "—", "—"],
  ["Saudi Arabia", "159", "—", "—"],
  ["Australia", "99", "—", "—"],
  ["United States", "77", "—", "—"],
];
---
<Base title="The name"
      eyebrow="646 in the world, against 53,764"
      dek="Booyzen is not a variant spelling that drifted. It is one family's deliberate break from a mass surname — and the distribution figures, gathered a century later and entirely independently of the family, say exactly that.">

<section class="blk">
  <h2>The claim being tested</h2>
  <blockquote>
    "There were some Booysens who were joiners, fighting on the side of the British, so our great
    grandfather <strong>changed the spelling to Booyzen</strong> so as not to be associated with them."
    <span class="cap" style="display:block;margin-top:8px">— family testimony recorded on the MyHeritage
    tree from Tersia Booyzen. <a href={u("/rebels/")}>The full account &rarr;</a></span>
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    <span class="chip doc">Documented</span> That a change happened is on the record twice over. MyHeritage
    carries <strong>"Former name: Booysen"</strong> as a formal Name Change fact on
    <strong>Willem Hermanus "William Henry" Booyzen (1851–1905)</strong> — and, far better, both spellings
    appear <strong>on a single sheet of paper in September 1905</strong>.
  </p>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin:22px 0">
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/detail-1905-clerk-booysen.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="1905 death form, clerk's hand, reading Willem Hermanus Booysen with an s." />
      <figcaption class="cap">The registering clerk names the dead man:
        <strong>Willem Hermanus Boo<em>y</em>sen</strong></figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/detail-1905-signature-booyzen.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="1905 death form, informant signature reading J B Booyzen with a clear z." />
      <figcaption class="cap">Four lines below, his son signs:
        <strong>J. B. Boo<em>y</em><em>z</em>en</strong></figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The informant is <strong>John Barry Booyzen</strong>, the eldest son, twenty-one, recorded as
    <em>present at the death</em>. His father is buried under the old spelling; he signs the new one.
    <a href={u("/documents/")}>The full document &rarr;</a>
  </p>
  <p>
    <span class="chip lore">Family lore</span> The <em>motive</em> — the joiners — is testimony, and
    cannot be proved from a spelling alone.
  </p>
  <p>
    But a deliberate break from a large surname should leave a very particular fingerprint in the
    population a century later: <strong>a tiny name, in the wrong province.</strong> That is precisely what
    is there.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>The complication: he may have been signing a z in 1879</h2>
  <figure style="margin:18px 0;max-width:520px">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1879-groom-signature.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Extreme magnification of an 1879 signature surname reading Booij, then an ambiguous letter, then en." />
    <figcaption class="cap">The groom's own signature on the 1879 marriage register, at maximum available
      magnification. The clerk, on the same page, wrote <strong>Booysen</strong>.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    He signed what reads as <strong>"Booij_en"</strong> — the older Dutch <strong>ij</strong> digraph, with
    a medial letter whose descending loop is more consistent with <strong>z</strong> than <strong>s</strong>.
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    If that reading is right, the z was already his own hand in <strong>1879</strong> — twenty years before
    the war — and the testimony explains a <em>motive</em> for something that had already happened.
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    <span class="chip inf">Not established</span> The reading is <strong>not certain</strong>. Cursive Dutch
    <em>s</em> and <em>z</em> are genuinely confusable, and this is a photograph of a duplicate register
    rather than the original. The archive states both readings and prefers neither.
  </p>
  <p>
    Note what it would and would not overturn. It would not touch the <strong>distribution evidence</strong>
    below — the name would still be a tiny, Gauteng-concentrated break from a Cape surname. It would move
    the <em>date</em> of the break back a generation, and make the joiners a remembered reason rather than
    the original one. <a href={u("/documents/")}>The register &rarr;</a>
  </p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>The control that changes what a z is worth</h2>
  <p>
    <span class="chip doc">Documented</span> The archive has now read the <strong>1851 Graaff-Reinet
    baptism register</strong> from the Cape Town Archives scan rather than from a photograph of it. Entry
    <strong>No. 2809</strong>, Willem Hermanus, born 4 May 1851, baptised 31 October 1851 at
    <em>Achter Sneeuwberg</em>. The father's name is written out, unambiguously, with a
    <strong>z</strong>:
  </p>
  <figure style="margin:18px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1851-father-booyzen.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="1851 baptism register, father's name reading Petrus Jacobus Booyzen with a clear z." />
    <figcaption class="cap">1851: <strong>Petrus Jacobus Boo<em>y</em><em>z</em>en</strong> — fifty-four
      years before the 1905 sheet, and in a clerk's hand, not the family's.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    Taken alone that would be a discovery: the z, already on paper, two generations early. It is not,
    and the same entry says why. Three lines below, the <strong>same clerk in the same ink</strong> writes
    the godparents' surname twice — and writes it the same way:
  </p>
  <figure style="margin:18px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1851-pieterzen-control.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="1851 baptism register witnesses, reading Willem Hermanus Pieterzen and Xaverius Hermanus Pieterzen, both with the same z letterform." />
    <figcaption class="cap">The witnesses: <strong>Willem Hermanus Pieter<em>z</em>en</strong> and
      <strong>Xaverius Hermanus Pieter<em>z</em>en</strong> — the identical letterform, the identical
      descender.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    <strong>Pietersen is not a family that changed its name.</strong> In Dutch orthography
    <b>-zen</b> is the ordinary way to write the patronymic ending, and this clerk wrote it that way for
    everybody who came to his font. The z in <em>Booyzen</em> here is the <strong>clerk's spelling
    convention, not the family's claim on it</strong>.
  </p>
  <div class="note">
    <p style="margin:0">
      <strong>What this costs the argument, and what it does not.</strong> It removes any reading in which
      the z was <em>invented</em> — in 1905, on the road north, or by any one man. The spelling was
      ordinary Cape-Dutch orthography and was lying about in the registers the whole time. It also
      weakens the <strong>1879 signature</strong> above: a z-shaped letter in a Dutch hand is no longer
      evidence of anything in particular.
    </p>
    <p style="margin:10px 0 0">
      It leaves the <strong>1905 sheet</strong> standing, because that document does not turn on the
      shape of a letter. It turns on <em>two different spellings of one man's name on one page</em> — the
      registering clerk writing <b>Booysen</b>, the son signing <b>Booyzen</b> four lines below. A
      divergence within a single document is still a divergence. And it leaves the
      <strong>distribution evidence</strong> below entirely untouched: 646 against 53,764, in the wrong
      province. Whatever the family did, they did it — the population figures are not a matter of
      handwriting.
    </p>
  </div>
  <p>
    <span class="chip inf">This archive's own correction</span> An earlier pass read this register from a
    photograph on the family tree and recorded the father as <em>Boo[i]j[s/z]en</em> — ambiguous — and the
    witnesses as <strong>Pieter<em>s</em>en</strong>, with an s. Both readings were wrong, and the second
    was the one that mattered: it hid the control. The archive scan settles both.
  </p>
</section>

<Rule name="awl" />
<section class="blk">
  <h2>The spelling is a hundred and twenty years older than the war</h2>
  <p class="dek">The 1851 register suggested it. The Cape Archives index settles it.</p>
  <p>
    The <strong>National Automated Archival Information Retrieval System</strong> was searched for the
    <b>z</b> spellings in the <em>Cape Town Archives Repository</em>. It is not a small number, and it does
    not start where the family story says it should:
  </p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin:20px 0">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Spelling</th><th class="m">Documents in KAB</th><th class="m">Earliest</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>BOOYZEN</strong></td><td class="m">39</td><td class="m"><strong>1779</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>BOOIJZEN</strong></td><td class="m">3</td><td class="m"><strong>1792</strong></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>The earliest is <span class="m">KAB CJ 2633: 29</span> — <b>"BOOYZEN, CHRISTINA. TESTAMENT. 1779."</b>
    Then a will in <b>1808</b>, a memorial to the Colonial Office in <b>1810</b>, a liquidation account in
    <b>1820</b>, a Stellenbosch testament in <b>1821</b>, and on without a gap through the nineteenth
    century.</p>
  <blockquote>
    The Anglo-Boer War began in <strong>1899</strong>. Somebody was already writing this surname with a
    <strong>z</strong> a hundred and twenty years before it.
  </blockquote>
  <div class="note">
    <p style="margin:0"><strong>Be precise about what this kills and what it leaves standing.</strong></p>
    <p style="margin:10px 0 0"><b>Killed:</b> any version of the story in which the <em>spelling itself</em>
      was created — invented by a great-grandfather, coined on the road north, made up to escape the
      joiners. It was ordinary Cape orthography before the family in question was born.</p>
    <p style="margin:10px 0 0"><b>Left standing:</b> that <em>this family</em> changed which of the two
      existing spellings it used. That is a different claim, and the <b>1905 death registration</b> still
      supports it — the clerk writes <b>Booysen</b> and the son signs <b>Booyzen</b> four lines below, on
      one sheet. Choosing an old spelling is still choosing.</p>
    <p style="margin:10px 0 0"><b>Untouched:</b> the distribution figures below. 646 against 53,764,
      concentrated in the wrong province. Whatever this family did, the population data still shows it.</p>
  </div>
  <h3 style="margin-top:34px">And a Booyzen, with a z, in Cape government service in 1904</h3>
  <p>Three files in the Attorney-General's series, all spelled with the <b>z</b>, two years after the war
    ended:</p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin:16px 0">
    <table>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="m" style="width:12rem">KAB AG 1393: 1547</td><td><b>"BOOYZEN, PJ. Leave of absence, Middelburg Police Office."</b></td><td class="m">1904</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">KAB AG 1648: 8946</td><td><b>"BOOYZEN, PJ. Constable. Application for leave. Middelburg."</b></td><td class="m">1905</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">KAB AG 1772: 11084</td><td><b>"BOOYZEN, PJ. Constable. Leave of. Middelburg."</b></td><td class="m">1907</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">KAB DOC 4/1/1075: 5377</td><td>Mortgage bond, Pieter Ignatius du Plessis and <b>Petrus Jacobus Booyzen</b></td><td class="m">1904</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">KAB DOC 4/1/1185: 1256</td><td>Mortgage bond, the same two men</td><td class="m">1906</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    <span class="chip inf">Not connected to this family — yet</span> <b>Petrus Jacobus</b> is this family's
    core male name, carried by three generations of it. <b>Middelburg</b> is the district where
    <b>Petrus Jacobus Booysen died at Zevenfontein in 1884</b>. And a man of that name, spelled with the
    <b>z</b>, is a <b>police constable there in 1904, 1905 and 1907</b>, holding mortgage bonds in 1904 and
    1906.
  </p>
  <p style="margin-top:12px">
    The archive cannot yet show he is a relative, and says so. But if he is, it is worth sitting with: the
    testimony says the spelling was changed to avoid being taken for men who served the British — and here
    is a Booyzen, spelled the new way, <b>in the Cape colonial police</b>.
    <a href={u("/open-questions/")}>Open questions &rarr;</a>
  </p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>One constable, one town, both spellings — and it weakens the 1905 sheet</h2>
  <p class="dek">The strongest single test of what these two spellings meant to the people writing them
    turns out to have nothing to do with this family at all.</p>
  <p>
    The Cape Attorney-General's registry files hold seven documents about a <b>police constable at
    Middelburg</b> between 1904 and 1907. They are plainly one man — one rank, one station, one run of
    leave applications. The registry clerks could not agree how to spell him:
  </p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin:20px 0">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th class="m" style="width:11rem">Reference</th><th>As filed</th><th class="m">Year</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="m">KAB AG 1393: 1547</td><td><b>BOOY<em>Z</em>EN, PJ.</b> Leave of absence, Middelburg Police Office</td><td class="m">1904</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">KAB AG 1543: 14632</td><td>Five pound note found by <b>PJ BOOY<em>S</em>EN</b>, Middelburg</td><td class="m">1904</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">KAB AG 1594: 3531</td><td><b>BOOY<em>S</em>EN, PJ.</b> Constable. Application for leave. Middelburg, Cape</td><td class="m">1905</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">KAB AG 1628: 6598</td><td><b>BOOY<em>S</em>EN, PJ.</b> Constable. Application for leave. Middelburg</td><td class="m">1905</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">KAB AG 1648: 8946</td><td><b>BOOY<em>Z</em>EN, PJ.</b> Constable. Application for leave. Middelburg</td><td class="m">1905</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">KAB AG 1659: 10381</td><td><b>BOOY<em>S</em>EN, PJ.</b> Constable. Application for leave. Middelburg</td><td class="m">1905</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">KAB AG 1772: 11084</td><td><b>BOOY<em>Z</em>EN, PJ.</b> Constable. Leave of. Middelburg</td><td class="m">1907</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <blockquote>
    Four times with an <strong>s</strong>, three times with a <strong>z</strong> — and <strong>twice both
    ways inside 1905</strong>, months apart, in the same registry, about the same man's leave.
  </blockquote>

  <div class="note">
    <p style="margin:0"><strong>What this does to the archive's own centrepiece.</strong> This site has
      leaned hard on the <b>1905 death registration</b>: the clerk writes <em>Booysen</em>, the son signs
      <em>Booyzen</em>, four lines apart. That has been read as the change caught in the act.</p>
    <p style="margin:10px 0 0">These seven files say that in <b>exactly those years</b>, in official Cape
      writing, <b>the two spellings were interchangeable labels for one person</b>. So the clerk's
      <em>Booysen</em> on the 1905 form is <b>not evidence of an older spelling being displaced</b>. It is
      what a Cape clerk in 1905 might write on any given day, for anybody.</p>
    <p style="margin:10px 0 0"><b>What survives is narrower, and it is the half that was always the
      stronger half:</b> the son <em>signed</em>. A clerk's hand records a clerk's habit; a signature
      records a man's own choice of how his name is spelled. <b>John Barry Booyzen chose the z.</b> The
      contrast with the clerk beside him is no longer the point.</p>
  </div>

  <p style="margin-top:20px">
    <span class="chip inf">And the constable is not shown to be family</span> <b>Petrus Jacobus</b> is
    this family's core male name and <b>Middelburg</b> is the district where Petrus Jacobus Booysen died
    at Zevenfontein in 1884 — and where his son <b>Gerrit Jacobus married in 1896</b> and had a son
    baptised <b>Booyzen</b> in 1904. The constable sits in the middle of that, and may well be one of
    them. The archive cannot show it, and says so.
    <a href={u("/open-questions/")}>Open questions &rarr;</a>
  </p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>How rare Booyzen is</h2>
  <p>
    Forebears, accessed 9 September 2026: <strong>646 bearers worldwide</strong>, the
    <strong>507,320th</strong> commonest surname on earth, an incidence of
    <strong>1 in 11,281,031</strong>. Ninety-six per cent are in Africa.
  </p>
  <Distribution rows={[["South Africa", 616], ["Australia", 16], ["United Arab Emirates", 6],
                        ["United States", 3], ["Botswana", 1], ["Ireland", 1], ["England", 1],
                        ["Saudi Arabia", 1], ["Turkey", 1]]} />
  <p class="cap" style="margin:6px 0 20px">Where the 646 Booyzens are. Ninety-five per cent of them are
    in one country.</p>
  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Country</th><th>Bearers</th><th>Incidence</th><th>Rank in country</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{zrows.map(([c, b, i, r]) => (
        <tr><td>{c}</td><td class="m">{b}</td><td class="m">{i}</td><td class="m">{r}</td></tr>))}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="cap">Note the sixteen in <strong>Australia</strong>. David Defranceski's own family is in
    Brisbane; the Booyzen descent reached it through the Lerena and Defranceski marriages rather than
    under the Booyzen name, so those sixteen are likely to be a separate migration. Unchecked.</p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>And how common Booysen is</h2>
  <ProvinceSplit />
  <p style="margin-top:16px">
    <strong>53,764 worldwide</strong>, the 10,459th commonest surname on earth, and
    <strong>52,009 of them in South Africa</strong> — one South African in a thousand.
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    <strong>Booysen is 83 times commoner than Booyzen.</strong><br />
    In South Africa the gap is wider still: <strong>1 in 1,042</strong> against <strong>1 in 87,951</strong>
    — a factor of <strong>84</strong>.
  </blockquote>
</section>

<Rule name="koppie" />
<section class="blk">
  <h2>The finding: the two names live in different provinces</h2>
  <p>This is the part that is hard to explain any other way.</p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Province</th><th>Share of <em>Booysen</em></th><th>Share of <em>Booyzen</em></th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Western Cape</strong></td><td class="m">39%</td><td class="m">10%</td>
          <td>the parent name's heartland</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Eastern Cape</strong></td><td class="m">17%</td><td class="m">—</td>
          <td>the old Cape Colony districts</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Gauteng</strong></td><td class="m">15%</td><td class="m"><strong>45%</strong></td>
          <td><strong>the Witwatersrand — Johannesburg</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Limpopo</td><td class="m">—</td><td class="m">9%</td><td>—</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:22px">
    <p><b>Booysen is a Cape surname.</b> Fifty-six per cent of its South African bearers are in the
      Western and Eastern Cape, which is where the family arrived in the seventeenth century and stayed.</p>
    <p><b>Booyzen is a Johannesburg surname.</b> Nearly half of all the Booyzens in the world are in
      <b>Gauteng</b> — a province that did not exist as a settlement until 1886.</p>
    <p>Now set that against the testimony, which was recorded without any reference to statistics:
      a family in the <b>Eastern Cape</b> changes its spelling during the war, is burned out, and
      <b>puts itself on a train to Johannesburg</b>, where the boys go onto the mines.</p>
    <blockquote>
      The surname data was not collected to test that story, and cannot have been shaped by it. It says
      the same thing: <strong>a small group broke off a Cape name and reproduced on the Rand.</strong>
    </blockquote>
    <p><span class="chip inf">Inferred</span> This is corroboration, not proof. Distribution cannot show
      <em>why</em> a spelling changed, and 616 people is a small enough number that founder effects and
      simple clerical drift both remain live explanations. What it does establish is that
      <b>Booyzen behaves like a young name with a single origin</b>, not like a long-standing spelling
      variant scattered through the Cape alongside its parent.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>What the name means</h2>
  <div class="grid g2">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">A patronymic</h3>
      <p><b>Booysen</b> is Dutch and means <b>"son of Booy"</b>. <em>Booy</em> is itself a short form of
        <b>Boudewijn</b> — the Dutch form of <b>Baldwin</b>, from Germanic <em>bald</em> ("bold") and
        <em>win</em> ("friend"). The English cousin of the name is <b>Boyce</b>; the North German and
        Danish is <b>Boysen</b>.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">A progenitor</h3>
      <p>The South African family descends from <b>Boij Boijeszoon</b> — modernised to <b>Boy Booysen</b> —
        who entered <b>Dutch East India Company</b> service giving his home as <b>Barlt, in Northern
        Friesland, Schleswig-Holstein</b>, and settled at <b>Stellenbosch and Drakenstein</b> in the
        mid-seventeenth century.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:22px">
    If that progenitor is right, then this family is <b>German or Frisian on the male line as well</b> —
    and joins <b>Kolbe</b> of Württemberg, <b>Schleher</b>, and the German Lutheran community of Soho on
    the female lines. <b>A family that reads as Afrikaans and English is, underneath, substantially
    German.</b> <span class="chip inf">Inferred</span> The link from Gerthardus Lodewikus Booysen to Boy
    Booysen is <b>not demonstrated in this archive</b>. <a href={u("/direct-line/")}>Where the line stops &rarr;</a>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>1942 &middot; A clerk who typed the z</h2>
  <p class="dek">The one document in this archive where an official, unprompted, spells it the family's
    way.</p>
  <figure style="margin:20px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1942-born-booyzen.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Question seven of the 1942 Daschner death notice, typed: Catherine Mary Sophia Daschner, born Booyzen." />
    <figcaption class="cap">Estate <strong>TAB MHG 1928/42</strong>, Johannesburg, April 1942 &mdash;
      <strong>"(born Booyzen)"</strong>.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Every other official spelling this archive has read runs the other way. The Indwe clerk in 1905
      wrote <b>Booysen</b> on Willem Hermanus's death form, and his son signed <b>Booyzen</b> four lines
      below it. The Middelburg files spell one constable both ways twice inside a single year. The
      Johannesburg registrar typed <b>Booysen</b> on Anna Pretorius's 1944 death registration.</p>
    <blockquote>Here, thirty-seven years after Indwe, a Johannesburg typist taking down a widow's
      account of her own maiden name types <b>Booyzen</b> &mdash; and there is no signature anywhere on
      the page to have copied it from. <b>The only source for the z was her saying it.</b></blockquote>
    <p>Set this beside the constable files and the position is stable in both directions: <b>a clerk's
      s proves nothing about the family, and a clerk's z proves nothing either.</b> What it does show is
      that by 1942 the z was what a Booyzen daughter said out loud when asked her name &mdash; which is
      a claim about the family, not about the archive. <a href={u("/documents/#daschner-1942")}>The
      document &rarr;</a></p>
    <p class="note"><b>And a caution that belongs on this page.</b> There is a Johannesburg suburb called
      <b>Booysens</b>, two miles from where this family lived in the 1940s. Any index search for the
      name in that city collects people whose only tie to the word is a street address &mdash; this
      archive met one, a miner called Rennie, before it recognised what it was looking at.
      <a href={u("/places/booysens/")}>Booysens &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>The family could not spell it either</h2>
  <p class="dek">These four spellings all appear inside this one family's records, sometimes among
    brothers and sisters.</p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Spelling</th><th>Where it appears here</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="m"><strong>Booysen</strong></td><td>the parent name; used by Gerthardus Lodewikus, both Petrus Jacobuses, and most of the nineteenth-century siblings</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m"><strong>Booyzen</strong></td><td>Willem Hermanus from the name change onward; the whole line since &mdash; and typed by a Johannesburg clerk in <strong>1942</strong>, from a daughter's dictation</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m"><strong>Booÿsen</strong></td><td><strong>Frederik Fortsenatus Booÿsen</strong>, b. 1899 — with the diaeresis, the older Cape Dutch orthography, in the year the war began</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m"><strong>Kolbe-Booysen</strong></td><td>a compound recorded for Maria Jacoba Smit, who married David Kolbe Booyzen in 1921</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-top:16px">Within a single sibling group the tree records <b>Jacobus Nicolaas Booysen</b>
    (b. 1840) and <b>Jacobus Nicolaas Booyzen</b> (b. 1841), both dying on <b>17 November 1882</b>. That is
    almost certainly <b>one man entered twice</b> under two spellings — a small demonstration of how
    easily this name splits. <a href={u("/disputed/")}>Disputed &rarr;</a></p>
  <p class="cap">Sources: Forebears (Booyzen; Booysen), accessed 9 September 2026; Wikipedia,
    <em>Booysen</em>; the MyHeritage tree <em>Defranceski Family Site (23andMe)</em>. Forebears figures are
    modelled estimates from name-frequency data, not a census, and should be read as orders of magnitude.</p>
</section>
